                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg_\infty^{op}
            may be very rich: its
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebras+on+infinity-stacks"} function algebra
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
            that are invariant under the canonical internal
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            are the
            %em closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differential"} Kähler differential
            forms on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Examples of derived spaces have appeared long ago as the configuration spaces in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+complex"} BV-BRST complex
            of a gauge theory is the function algebra on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            approximation to a derived orbifold whose internal symmetries are the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            of the locus of solutions to the physical equations of motion.
          %p
            More recently, much of the motivation for derived geometry came from the observation that the
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0910.5130"} Goerss-Hopkins-Miller theorem
            suggests that there is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Survey+of+Elliptic+Cohomology+-+the+derived+moduli+stack+of+derived+elliptic+curves"} derived moduli space of derived elliptic curves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} structure ∞-sheaf
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E-rings"} E-∞-rings
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section"} global section
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectrum"} ring spectrum
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
          %h2#plan Plan
          %p The rough plan is that we
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                talk about some relevant leftover topics from the later chapters of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
                = succeed ";" do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    last time
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Seminar+on+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Categories+and+%E2%88%9E-Stacks"} ?
            %li
              %p
                then discuss some of the specifics of
                = succeed "-sites" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %li
              %p
                and finally look at the specific case of derived
                %em smooth differential
                geometry.
          %p The following is the beginning of a detailed schedule of talks. The first few talks have been fixed by now, the later talks will be fixed as we move along.
          %h3#Sites
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-Sites from
            = succeed "-algebraic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em Oct 15, 2010
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
                \/
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theories"} theories
                of
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-%E2%88%9E-ring"} C-∞-ring
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
                famously noticed that these theories are encoded by categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                with products, all whose objects are cartesian powers of a generating object
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+Lawvere+theory"} algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                of a Lawvere theory is identified with a product-preserving co-presheaf
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      A : T \to Set
                      \\,.
              %p
                Here the value
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(x)
                is identified with the underlying set of the algebra and for any morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : x^n \to x
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                the morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(f) : A(x^n) \simeq A(x)^n \to A(x)
                is an
                = succeed "-ary" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                encodes the compatibilities of all these operations, such as associativity.
              %p
                The identification of algebras with co-presheaves gives rise to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %msup
                        %mi Alg
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg^{op}
                of the category of all
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(C)
                on a small full subcategory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi T
                      %msup
                        %mi Alg
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \hookrightarrow T Alg^{op}
                is the context in which geometry over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                takes place. For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo =
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T =
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
                the theory of
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+algebra"} smooth algebra
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            %li
              %p
                %em Oct 22, 2010
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-algebraic+theory"} (∞,1)-algebraic theory
              %p
                There is an
                = succeed "-categorical" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+Lawvere+theory"} algebra over a Lawvere theory
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
                \.
              %p The idea is evident: we say
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    an
                    %strong
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                      \-algebraic theory
                    is an
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                    with
                    = succeed "s." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-limit"} (∞,1)-product
                %li
                  %p
                    an
                    %strong
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                      \-algebra
                    over
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                    is an
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo :
                          %mi T
                          %mo →
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A : T \to
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
                    that preserves these products;
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    = succeed "-category" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                    = succeed "-algebras" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi T
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sub-%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} sub-(∞,1)-category
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi T
                          %mi Alg
                          %mo ↪
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi Func
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi T
                          %mo ,
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi Grpd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty T Alg \hookrightarrow \infty Func(T, \infty Grpd)
                    of the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-presheaves"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-copresheaves
                    on those functors that preserve products.
            %li
              %p
                %em Dec 17, 2010
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-algebraic+theory+of+E-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} (2,1)-algebraic theory of E-∞ algebras
              %p
                An important example of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-algebraic+theory"} (∞,1)-algebraic theory
                beyond ordinary 1-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (2,1)-category
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-algebra+over+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-algebraic+theory"} algebras
                turn out to be
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} E-∞ algebra
              %p This is the result of
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Cranch"}> James Cranch
                  ,
                  %em
                    Algebraic Theories and
                    = succeed "-Categories" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                  (
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1011.3243"}> arXiv
                  )
          %p
            Next, in order to handle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi T
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty T Alg
            and to compare it to other known structures it is useful to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} present
            it in terms of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
          %h3#Models
            Models for
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em Oct 29, 2010
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+T-algebra"}> homotopy T-algebra
                  s
              %p
                We know from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
                that every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi Grpd
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A : T \to \infty Grpd
                is modeled by a strict functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
                %strong
                  homotopy
                  = succeed "-algebra" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                is such a functor that preserves products up to weak equivalence in that for all
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
                the canonical morphism
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munderover
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %mi n
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\prod_{i = 1}^n A(p_i) : A(x^n) \to (A(x))^n
              %p
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} weak equivalence
              %p
                There is a model structure for such homotopy
                = succeed "-algebras," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization+of+model+categories"} Bousfield localization
                of the projective
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
                at the morphisms
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                          %mi n
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_n F_T(1) \to F_T(n)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_T(n)
                is the free
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                generators.
              %p
                But it turns out that one can further strictify this: a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+algebra"} simplicial T-algebra
                is a strict functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mi sSet
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to sSet
                that also preserves all products up to isomorphism. There is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+T-algebras"} model structure on simplicial T-algebras
              %p
                And there is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
                between these model structures for homotopy
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "-algebras." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %p This Quillen equivalence is described in
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bernard+Badzioch"}> Bernard Badzioch
                  ,
                  %em Algebraic theories in homotopy theory
                  Annals of Mathematics, 155 (2002), 895-913 (
                  %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/3062135"}> JSTOR
                  )
              %p
                The result that strict simplicial algebras model all
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Julie+Bergner"}> Julie Bergner
                  ,
                  %em Rigidification of algebras over multi-sorted theories
                  , Algebraic and Geometric Topoogy 7, 2007.
            %li
              %p
                %em Nov 5, Nov 19, 2010
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence
                and
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebras"} model structure on dg-algebras
              %p
                We have seen that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+T-algebras"} model structure on simplicial T-algebras
                models
                = succeed "-T-algebras." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                is an
                %em abelian
                Lawvere theory, so that every
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} normalized chains complex
                of a simplicial
                = succeed "-algebra:" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
                this is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
                (in non-negative degree).
              %p
                There is a standard
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebra"} model structure on dg-algebra
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence
                asserts that at least for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                a theory of ordinary commutative algebras, there is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Alg
                        %mi proj
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Δ
                            %mi op
                      %mo ≃
                      %msubsup
                        %mi dgAlg
                        %mi proj
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      T Alg_{proj}^{\Delta^{op}} \simeq dgAlg^+_{proj}
              %p
                between the model structure on simplicial algebras and that on chain dg-algebras in non-negative degree. This is a monoidal refinement of the standard
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
                that identifies
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+abelian+group"} simplicial abelian group
                = succeed "es." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Quillen+adjunction"} monoidal Quillen adjunction
              %p
                This means that dg-algebras are yet another equivalent model for (certain)
                = succeed "-algebras." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %li
              %p
                %em Nov 12, 2010
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+algebras+over+an+operad"} model structure on algebras over an operad
              %p
                Algebraic theories may also be encoded by
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-algebras+over+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-operad"} ∞-algebras over an (∞,1)-operad
                = succeed "-structure" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+algebras+over+an+operad"} model structure on algebras over an operad
              %p
                Many classical examples of
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} A-∞ algebra
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+space"} A-∞ space
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} E-∞ algebra
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
          %h3#Smooth
            Derived geometry over
            = succeed "-rings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty-C^\infty
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em Nov 27, Dec 3, 2010
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+loop+space"}> derived loop space
                  s
              %p
                Analogous to how a good deal of the phenomenology of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} weak quotient
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *//G
                we have that much of the phenomenology of derived geometry is exhibited already by
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space+object"} free loop space object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
                of a derived space
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-pullback"} (∞,1)-pullback
                of the diagonal on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                along itself
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo ×
                          %mi X
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathcal{L}X := X \times_{X \times X} X
                      \\,.
              %p
                When
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is an ordinary object in the inclusion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %msup
                        %mi Alg
                        %mi op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Alg
                        %mn ∞
                        %mi op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)Sh(T Alg^{op}) \hookrightarrow (\infty,1)Sh(T Alg_\infty^{op})
                one calls
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
                for emphasis also the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+loop+space"} derived loop space
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %p
                Since this pullback is maximally non-transversal, the derived free loop space is quite different from the ordinary free loop space object computed in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi Sh
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi T
                        %msup
                          %mi Alg
                          %mi op
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)Sh(T Alg^{op})
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is 0-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated"} truncated
                (just a plain space, no groupoidal morphisms, no derived resoluton) its underived loop space object is just
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                itself and hence uninteresting, whereas its derived loop space is very rich:
              %p
                one finds that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebras+on+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} function algebra on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
                is the complex that computes the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
                of the function algebra of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi HH
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      C(\mathcal{L}X) \simeq \mathbf{HH}_\bullet(C(X))
                      \\,.
              %p
                To some extent this is just the tautological dual reformulation of Hochschild homology as the
                = succeed "-categorical" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi HH
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi A
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathbf{HH}_\bullet(A) := A \otimes_{A \otimes A} A
                      \\,.
              %p
                But there are some noteworthy subtleties. For instance in the traditional literature the derived tensor product is taken in the context of modules, not of algebras. But one can see that forming a fibrant replacement in the model structure on homotopy
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %p This relation between derived loop spaces and Hochschild homology is very fruitful. It gives a transparent conceptual interpretation to many constructions in Hochschild cohomology and makes all this standard theory applicable to the study of derived geometry.
            %li
              %p
                %em Dec 10 19, 2010
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+smooth+manifold"}> derived smooth manifold
                  s
              %p
                The Lawvere theory that encodes standard models of smooth differential geometry (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"}> synthetic differential geometry
                ) is the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+algebra"} smooth algebra
                = succeed "-rings." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed "-rings." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
              %p
                Spaces locally ringed in such smooth
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %em derived smooth manifolds
                \.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+differential+geometry"} derived differential geometry
          %h2#references References
          %p
            A systematic description of derived geometry using
            = succeed "-theoretic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+To%C3%ABn"}> Bertrand Toën
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriele+Vezzosi"} Gabriele Vezzosi
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Homotopical Algebraic Geometry I: Topos theory
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0207028"}> arXiv
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Homotopical Algebraic Geometry II: geometric stacks and applications
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0404373"}> arXiv
                    )
          %p
            This generalizes the Brown-Jardine-Joyal-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+sSet-enriched+presheaves"} model structure on sSet-enriched presheaves
            over an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet-site"} sSet-site
          %p
            A proposal for the precise set of the scene of derived geometry in general abstract
            = succeed "-terms" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"} Structured Spaces
          %p
            The main point of this is a formalization and identification of special tame objects inside the collection of all
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+scheme"} derived scheme
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} structured (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            The relevance of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+loop+space"} derived loop space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ben-Zvi"} David Ben-Zvi
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Nadler"} David Nadler
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ben-Zvi"}> David Ben-Zvi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Nadler"} David Nadler
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Loop Spaces and Langlands Parameters
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.0322"}> arXiv:0706.0322
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Integral Transforms and Drinfeld Centers in Derived Algebraic Geometry
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0805.0157"}> arXiv:0805.0157
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Loop Spaces and Connections
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1002.3636"}> arXiv:1002.3636
                    )
                  %p
                    This article uses Toën’s theory of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebras+on+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} function algebras on ∞-stacks
                    for showing that the function complex on a derived loop space
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℒ
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
                    is under mild conditions the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
                    complex of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                    hence by
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild-Kostant-Rosenberg+theorem"} Hochschild-Kostant-Rosenberg theorem
                    the collection of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differential"} Kähler differential
                    forms on
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℒ
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
                    that are invariant under the canonical
                    = succeed "-" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                    on
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℒ
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
                    are the
                    %em closed
                    forms. This also gives a geometric interpretation of the old observation by
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
                    and others, that the differential and grading on the de Rham complex may be understood as induced from automorphisms of the
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/odd+line"} odd line
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Loop Spaces and Representations
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1004.5120"}> arXiv:1004.5120
                    )
          %p
            The application of derived geometry to the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            is described in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Survey+of+Elliptic+Cohomology"} A Survey of Elliptic Cohomology
          %p The article
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Spivak"}> David Spivak
              ,
              %em Derived smooth manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0810.5174"}> arXiv:0810.5174
              )
          %p
            discussed the
            = succeed "-version" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+algebra"} C-oo-rings
            – the algebras over the Lawvere theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            – and the corresponding locally ringed spaces:
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+smooth+manifold"} derived smooth manifold
          %p Some of the above material is taken from the seminar notes
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Seminar+on+derived+differential+geometry"} Seminar on derived differential geometry
              (2010)
          %p
            Survey in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luigi+Alfonsi"}> Luigi Alfonsi
              , §5 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Higher geometry in physics
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+Mathematical+Physics+2nd+ed"} Encyclopedia of Mathematical Physics 2nd ed
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.07308"} arXiv:2312.07308
